      \field{abstract}{Homomorphism indistinguishability is a way of characterising many natural equivalence relations on graphs. Two graphs \$G\$ and \$H\$ are called homomorphism indistinguishable over a graph class \${\textbackslash}mathcal\{F\}\$ if for each \$F {\textbackslash}in {\textbackslash}mathcal\{F\}\$, the number of homomorphisms from \$F\$ to \$G\$ equals the number of homomorphisms from \$F\$ to \$H\$. Examples of such equivalence relations include isomorphism and cospectrality, as well as equivalence with respect to many formal logics. Quantum groups are a generalisation of topological groups that describe "non-commutative symmetries" and, inter alia, have applications in quantum information theory. An important subclass are the easy quantum groups, which enjoy a combinatorial characterisation and have been fully classified by Raum and Weber. A recent connection between these seemingly distant concepts was made by Man{\textbackslash}v\{c\}inska and Roberson, who showed that quantum isomorphism, a relaxation of classical isomorphism that can be phrased in terms of the quantum symmetric group, is equivalent to homomorphism indistinguishability over the class of planar graphs. We generalise Man{\textbackslash}v\{c\}inska and Roberson's result to all orthogonal easy quantum groups. We obtain for each orthogonal easy quantum group a graph isomorphism relaxation \${\textbackslash}approx\$ and a graph class \${\textbackslash}mathcal\{F\}\$, such that homomorphism indistinguishability over \${\textbackslash}mathcal\{F\}\$ coincides with \${\textbackslash}approx\$. Our results include a full classification of the \$(0, 0)\$-intertwiners of the graph-theoretic quantum group obtained by adding the adjacency matrix of a graph to the intertwiners of an orthogonal easy quantum group.}
