      \field{labelnamesource}{author}
      \field{labeltitlesource}{title}
      \field{abstract}{We show that the existence of an oddomorphism from a graph \$F\$ to a graph \$G\$ does not imply that \$G\$ is a minor of \$F\$. This answers a question posed by Roberson (2022) and shows that the CFI graphs cannot be used to prove the Strong Roberson Conjecture. Additionally, we introduce the concept of a split-off minor and show that the existence of an oddomorphism from \$F\$ to \$G\$ implies that \$G\$ is a split-off minor of \$F\$. Consequently, every class that is closed under taking split-off minors and disjoint unions is homomorphism distinguishing closed. The split-off minor relation is the first minor-like structural relation shown to have this property, marking a meaningful advancement in our understanding of the interaction between structural graph containment and homomorphism indistinguishability relations.}
      \field{annotation}{Comment: 22 pages}
      \field{month}{7}
      \field{note}{arXiv:2607.03405 [cs.DM]}
      \field{title}{Oddomorphisms, {Split}-{Off} {Minors}, and the {Strong} {Roberson} {Conjecture}}
